#{[17592186054347 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the point of intersection of the two lines.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the solution should lie between $x=13$ and $x=14$. So, we can disregard all values of $x$ that are not between $13$ and $14$.\n\nNow, we evaluate the table entries for $x=13.2$ and $x=13.6$, as follows:\n\n- Substituting $x=13.2$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =0.43x+6 \\\\  & =0.43(13.2)+6 \\\\  & =11.676 \\\\ {y}_{2} & =0.65x+3 \\\\  & =0.65(13.2)+3 \\\\  & =11.58\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}| & =|11.676-11.58| \\\\  & =|0.096| \\\\  & =0.096\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Substituting $x=13.6$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =0.43x+6 \\\\  & =0.43(13.6)+6 \\\\  & =11.848 \\\\ {y}_{2} & =0.65x+3 \\\\  & =0.65(13.6)+3 \\\\  & =11.84\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}| & =|11.848-11.84| \\\\  & =|0.008| \\\\  & =0.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe fill this information into the table:\n\n| $x$ | $13.2$ | $13.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=0.43x+6$ | $11.676$ | $11.848$ |\n| $y_2=0.65x+3$ | $11.58$ | $11.84$ |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $0.096$ | $0.008$ |\n\nThe smallest error is $0.008$, which corresponds to $x=13.6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx\\approx 13.6\n$$\n\nis our approximate solution."] [17592186054415 :question/worked-solution "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms. In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(6a,30) = 6$, so:\n$\\begin{aligned}6a+30 &= (6\\cdot a)+(6\\cdot 5)\\end{aligned}$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}(6\\cdot a)+(6\\cdot 5) &= 6(a+5)\\end{aligned}$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $6(a+5)$."] [17592186054416 :question/worked-solution "Both terms are negative, so we begin by factoring out a negative sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5a - 35 &= - (5a + 35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we continue factoring in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms. In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(5a,35) = 5$, so\n$\\begin{aligned}-(5a+35) &= -(5\\cdot a+5\\cdot 7)\\end{aligned}$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}-(5\\cdot a+5\\cdot 7) &= -5(a+7)\\end{aligned}$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $-5(a+7)$."] [17592186054417 :question/worked-solution "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(27a, 33b, 30) = 3$, so:\n$\\begin{aligned}27a+33b-30 &= 3\\cdot 9a+3\\cdot 11b-3\\cdot 10\\end{aligned}$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 9a+3\\cdot 11b-3\\cdot 10 &= 3(9a+11b-10)\\end{aligned}$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $3(9a+11b-10)$."] [17592186055961 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi/2}\\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{2\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nIf we attempt to evaluate this limit by directly substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π/2}\\frac{\\sin 2x}{2\\cos x} &= (\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}))/(2\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= (\\sin (π))/(2\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before attempting to evaluate the limit, we obtain a more clear result.\n\nTo simplify the fraction in this particular case, the double angle formula for sine will be useful:\n\n$$\n\\sin{2x}=2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the double angle formula above, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   2 x}{2 \\cos   x}} & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cos   x}{2 \\cos   x}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cancel{\\cos   x}}{2 \\cancel{\\cos   x}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} \\sin   x \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\sin   ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nIn other cases, we may need to use a different trigonometric identity."] [17592186055964 :tutorial/content "It's possible to perform arithmetic with functions, just like with numbers. Shown below are the four arithmetic operations, addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\cdot)$, and division $(/)$, applied to the functions\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4x + 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = x - 2\n$$\n\n- **Addition**\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned} (f + g)(x) &= f(x) + g(x) \\\\ &= (4x + 1) + (x - 2) \\\\ &= 4x + 1 + x - 2 \\\\ &= 5x - 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Subtraction**\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(4x+1)-(x-2) \\\\  & =4x+1-x+2 \\\\  & =3x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Multiplication**\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =f(x)g(x) \\\\  & =(4x+1)(x-2) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-8x+x-2 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-7x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Division**\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(x) & =\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x+1}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In the case of division, we must have that\n\n$$\ng(x)\\neq 0\n$$\n\nso that we avoid dividing by zero."] [17592186056027 :tutorial/content "**Factoring** is the opposite of distributing. To illustrate, consider the expression $4(a-2b)$. If you consider distributing as going forward,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(a - 2b) &= 4 \\cdot a - 4 \\cdot 2b \\\\ &= 4a - 8b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen factoring would be going in reverse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a - 8b &= (4 \\cdot a)-(4 \\cdot 2b) \\\\ &= 4(a - 2b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, you would say that $\\color{red}4$ was **factored out** of $4a-8b$.\n\nTo factor any expression, we just need to follow these two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(4a,8b) = {\\color{red}{4}}$, so:\n$\\begin{aligned}4a-8b &= ({\\color{red}{4}}\\cdot a)-({\\color{red}{4}}\\cdot 2b)\\end{aligned}$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}({\\color{red}{4}}\\cdot a)-({\\color{red}{4}}\\cdot 2b) &= {\\color{red}{4}}(a-2b)\\end{aligned}$"] [17592186056069 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+12 & =13 & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 2+6 & =8 & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 3+4 & =7 & {\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+7x+12 &= {x}^{2}+\\underbrace{3x+4x}_{7x}+12\\end{aligned}$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x+4x+12 &= ({x}^{2}+3x)+(4x+12) \\\\ &= x(x+3)+4(x+3) \\\\ &= (x+4)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)(x+3) & =x(x+3)+4(x+3) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+4x+12 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+7x+12\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059611 :question/worked-solution "We start by using the definition of congruence modulo $n$. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n$a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a-4 \\equiv b-4 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)$\n\nSo, we can reason as follows:\n\n> Since $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)$, by the definition of modular congruence,\n> $7 \\mid (a-b) \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad a-b = 7k$\n> for some integer $k$.\n\nNow, we express the difference between $a-4$ and $b-4$ through the difference between $a$ and $b{:}$\n\n> Now, notice that\n> $\\begin{aligned}(a-4)-(b-4) & =a-4-b+4 \\\\  & =a-b \\\\  & =7k.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of congruence in reverse:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of congruence, we have\n> $a-4 \\equiv b-4 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)$."] [17592186059612 :question/worked-solution "We start by using the definition of congruent numbers. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n$a \\equiv b \\: \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 8a \\equiv 8b \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$\n\nSo, we can reason as follows:\n\n> Since $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$, by the definition of modular congruence,\n> $10 \\mid (a-b) \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad a - b = 10k$\n> for some integer $k$.\n\nThe equation above involves the difference between $a$ and $b$. But we want a relationship between $8a$ and $8b$. To get an expression involving $8a$ and $8b$, we can multiply the above equation by $8{:}$\n\n> Now, multiplying both sides of $a - b = 10k$ by $8$, we get\n> $\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot (a-b) & =8\\cdot 10k \\\\ 8a-8b & =10(8k) \\\\ 8a-8b & =10m,\\end{aligned}$,\n> where $m = 8k$ is an integer.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence in reverse:\n\n> According to the definition of modular congruence, the final equation implies that\n> $8a \\equiv 8b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$."] [17592186060900 :tutorial/content "The following identities are called the **cofunction identities**. We can split them into three main groups.\n\n- Sine and **co**sine:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}x & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}x & =\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Tangent and **co**tangent:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}x & =\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}x & =\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secant and **co**secant:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}x & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}x & =\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060918 :tutorial/content "The dot product satisfies many properties that we would normally expect multiplication to satisfy. Given the vectors $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c}$ and any scalar $\\lambda$, the following properties hold.\n\n- The dot product distributes over addition:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b})\\cdot \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{c}+\\mathbf{b}\\cdot \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The dot product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The dot product is commutative:\n$\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$"] [17592186060981 :tutorial/content "Let's construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{a \\equiv b}_{P(a,b)} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{a+2 \\equiv b+2}_{Q(a,b)} \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)\n$$\n\nWe start by assuming the antecedent $P$ is true.\n\n> *Assume that* $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)$.\n\nNext, we state what this means using the definition of congruence.\n\n> *By the definition of modular congruence,*\n> $5 \\mid (a-b) \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad a-b = 5k$\n> *for some integer $k$.*\n\nNow, the goal is to show that\n\n$$\n(a+2) \\equiv (b+2) \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad 5 \\mid \\big((a+2) - (b+2)\\big)\n$$\n\nSo, we write down the difference between $(a+2)$ and $(b+2)$ and show that it is a multiple of $5$ using our assumption regarding the difference between $a$ and $b{:}$\n\n> *Hence, we get*\n> $\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-(b+2) & =a+2-b-2 \\\\  & =a-b \\\\  & =5k.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nSince $(a+2) - (b+2)$ is a multiple of $5$, this shows that $(a+2) \\equiv (b+2)$ modulo $5$. So, our proof is complete, and we write our conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, according to the definition of congruence, we have*\n> $a+2 \\equiv b+2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)$.\n\nNow that we've discussed all the details, let's write down our final proof."] [17592186061011 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a+2 \\equiv b+2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)\n$$\n\nProof:\n\n> *Assume that* $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)$. *By the definition of modular congruence,*\n> $5 \\mid (a-b) \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad a-b = 5k$\n> *for some integer $k$. Hence, we get*\n> $\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-(b+2) & =a+2-b-2 \\\\  & =a-b \\\\  & =5k.\\end{aligned}$.\n> *Therefore, according to the definition of congruence, we have*\n> $a+2 \\equiv b+2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)$."] [17592186064105 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= g(x) \\\\\nx^{3} + x^{2} + 1 &= x^{2} + x + 1 \\\\\nx^{3} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: $A_1$ and $A_2$.\n\n![](images/03/03ec1d82eb7375ce3f39a6e55d2271125d55db3fe957ac2c6a97f4dc6754de8d.png)\n\n- For the area $A_1$, we have $x \\in [-1,0]$, and the upper function is $f(x)$ while the lower function is $g(x)$. So, the area is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(f(x)-g(x))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1)-({x}^{2}+x+1))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}){|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =0-\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area $A_2$, we have $x \\in [0,1]$, and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x)$. So, the area is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(g(x)-f(x))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(({x}^{2}+x+1)-({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{1} + A_{2} = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186064106 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\n\\color{black}f(y)=y\\,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\, \\color{black}g(y)= y^{1/7}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y) &= g(y) \\\\\ny &= y^{1/7} \\\\\ny^{7} &= y \\\\\ny(y^{6} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\ny(y^{3} - 1)(y^{3} + 1) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: $A_1$ and $A_2$.\n\n![](images/36/3694f17ac55028c3449956601acf52f49ed840b68c47258a0b800bb56e5fd3df.png)\n\n- For the area $A_1$, we have $y \\in [-1,0]$, and the right function is $f(y)$ while the left function is $g(y)$. So, the area is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(f(y)-g(y))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(y-{y}^{1/7})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-\\frac{7}{8}{y}^{8/7}){|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{7}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area $A_2$, we have $y \\in [0,1]$, and the right function is $g(y)$ while the left function is $f(y)$. So, the area is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(g(y)-f(y))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{1/7}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(\\frac{7}{8}{y}^{8/7}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{7}{8}-\\frac{1}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{1} + A_{2} = \\frac{3}{8} + \\frac{3}{8} = \\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186064130 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=-x^5\n$$\n\nand $g(x)=-x$. First, we find the points where the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= g(x) \\\\\n- x^{5} &= -x \\\\\nx^{5} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{4} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 1)(x^{2} + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 1)(x + 1)(x^{2} + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -1, 0, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: $A_1$ and $A_2$.\n\n![](images/d9/d981231eabfdab75ef1581d21ae41e218225f1603fe5e424d06ec1fbda47ab62.png)\n\n- For the area $A_1$, we have $x \\in [-1,0]$, and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x)$. So, the area is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[g(x)-f(x)]\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(-x)-(-{x}^{5})]\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area $A_2$, we have $x \\in [0,1]$, and the upper function is $f(x)$ while the lower function is $g(x)$. So, the area is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[f(x)-g(x)]\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(-{x}^{5})-(-x)]\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{5})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{1} + A_{2} \\\\\n&= \\int_{-1}^{0}(x^{5} - x)dx + \\int_{0}^{1}(x - x^{5})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186065340 :tutorial/content "To factor a polynomial expression like $2a^2+6a$, we follow the three steps below:\n\n1. First, we find the greatest common factor (or $\\textrm{GCF}$) of both terms. In this case,\n$\\textrm{GCF}(2a^2,6a) = 2a$.\n2. Then, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\textrm{GCF}$:\n$\\begin{aligned}2{a}^{2}+6a &= (2a\\cdot a)+(2a\\cdot 3)\\end{aligned}$\n3. Finally, we write the $\\textrm{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n$\\begin{aligned}({\\color{blue}{2a}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{a}})+({\\color{blue}{2a}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{3}}) &= {\\color{blue}{2a}}\\hspace{0.167em}({\\color{red}{a}}+{\\color{red}{3}})\\end{aligned}$\n\nTherefore, the factored form of $2a^2+6a$ is $2a(a+3)$.\n\nWe can check that we factored correctly by multiplying it out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a(a + 3) &= 2a \\cdot a + 2a \\cdot 3 \\\\ &= 2a^{2} + 6a \\mid ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186068445 :question/worked-solution "We start by isolating the absolute value on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid 2 - 6q \\mid + 2q &= 6 \\\\\n\\mid 2 - 6q \\mid &= -2q + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two cases:\n\n- If $\\mid 2-6q|=2-6q$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2-6q| & =-2q+6 \\\\ 2-6q & =-2q+6 \\\\ 2 & =4q+6 \\\\ -4 & =4q \\\\ -1 & =q \\\\ q & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2-6q| & =-2q+6 \\\\ |2-6(-1)| & =-2(-1)+6 \\\\ |2+6| & =2+6 \\\\ |8| & =8 \\\\ 8 & =8\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $q=-1$ satisfies the original equation, it's a valid solution.\n- If $\\mid 2-6q|=-(2-6q)$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2-6q| & =-2q+6 \\\\ -(2-6q) & =-2q+6 \\\\ -2+6q & =-2q+6 \\\\ 8q-2 & =6 \\\\ 8q & =8 \\\\ q & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it actually solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2-6q| & =-2q+6 \\\\ |2-6(1)| & =-2(1)+6 \\\\ |2-6| & =-2+6 \\\\ |-4| & =4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $q=1$ satisfies the original equation, it's a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, our equation has two solutions $q=-1$ and $q=1$."] [17592186068532 :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - 4y^{2} &= 2x \\\\\n4x - 4y^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n4x &= 4y^{2} \\\\\nx &= y^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, from the second equation, we have\n\n$$\nx = 2y^2 - 5y\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= y^{2} \\\\\n2y^{2} - 5y &= y^{2} \\\\\ny^{2} - 5y &= 0 \\\\\ny(y - 5) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y$ into $x=y^2$.\n\n- For $y_1=0$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={y}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $y_2=5$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)^{2} \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = (0,0) \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad (x_2,y_2) = (25,5)\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require the solution such that\n\n$$\ny \\ne 0\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{y} &= \\frac{x_{2}}{y_{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{5} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186068603 :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = \\sqrt{x + 1} \\\\ y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{x}{2}} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and then solving for $x$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x + 1} &= \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx + 1 &= (\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{1}{2})^{2} \\\\\nx + 1 &= \\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n4x + 4 &= x^{2} + 2x + 1 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 1)(x - 3) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -1, 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we're dealing with square roots, we need to plug each of these solutions back into\n\n$$\n\\sqrt {x+1}=\\dfrac{x}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nto check whether it satisfies the original equation.\n\n- For $x=-1$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-1+1} & =\\frac{-1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & =0,\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nso $x=-1$ is indeed a solution of $\\sqrt {x+1}=\\dfrac{x}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n- For $x=3$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3+1} & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 & =2,\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nso $x=3$ is indeed a solution of $\\sqrt {x+1}=\\dfrac{x}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\nWe found that both $x=-1$ and $x=3$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n\\sqrt {x+1}=\\dfrac{x}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the radical curve, since it looks simpler.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{-1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,0)$.\n- Substituting $x=3$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nso the other point of intersection is $(3,2)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the points of intersection of the two curves are $(-1,0)$ and $(3,2)$.\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/b4/b4444f4cbb83e5a9d55f6d654a25b7b79ff8a2f02dc6a36d290e5fdf1da42da2.png)"] [17592186068652 :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinates of the intersections of\n\n$$\ny= \\mid 2x+3 \\mid\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=x+3\n$$\n\nwe need to solve the following equation for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mid 2x+3 \\mid = x+3\n$$\n\nThe absolute value graph\n\n$$\ny= \\mid 2x+3 \\mid\n$$\n\nhas two branches:\n\n- where $\\mid 2x+3 \\mid = -(2x+3)$, which has a negative slope, and\n- where $\\mid 2x+3 \\mid = 2x+3$, which has a positive slope.\n\nNow, let's consider each branch in turn:\n\n- The point $A$ lies on the branch with a negative slope. So, if $\\mid 2x+3 \\mid = -(2x+3)$, we obtain:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(2x+3) & =x+3 \\\\ -2x-3 & =x+3 \\\\ -3 & =3x+3 \\\\ -6 & =3x \\\\ -2 & =x \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x+3| & =x+3 \\\\ |2(-2)+3| & =(-2)+3 \\\\ |-4+3| & =1 \\\\ |-1| & =1 \\\\ 1 & =1\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=-2$ satisfies the original equation, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $x=-2$.\n\n- The point $B$ lies on the branch with a positive slope. So, if $\\mid 2x+3 \\mid = 2x+3$, we obtain:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =x+3 \\\\ x+3 & =3 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x+3| & =x+3 \\\\ |2(0)+3| & =(0)+3 \\\\ |0+3| & =3 \\\\ |3| & =3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=0$ satisfies the original equation, the $x$-coordinate of $B$ is $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$, respectively, are $-2$ and $0$."] [17592186069507 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}.\n$$"] [17592186069930 :tutorial/content "Let's consider our system of equations once more:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {x}^{2} - 5 x + 6 \\\\ y = 2\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNotice that this system contains two variables $x$ and $y$, and consists of one linear and one quadratic equation.\n\nWe'll now summarize the steps we took to find the solution:\n\n- **Step 1**: First, we used the method of substitution to eliminate the variable $y$, giving the following quadratic equation in the variable $x{:}$\n$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}$\n- **Step 2**: Next, we found that the solutions to this equation were\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = 1 \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad x = 4.\n$$\n- **Step 3**: Finally, we found the value of $y$ corresponding to each $x$-value by substituting back into one of the original equations. In this case, we found that $y = 2$ for both values of $x$, giving the solutions\n$$\n\\displaystyle (x_1,y_1) = (1,2) \\quad\\text{and}\\quad (x_2,y_2) = (4,2).\n$$\n\nThis particular system had two unique solutions in total. The number of solutions to a system consisting of one linear and one quadratic equation depends upon the *discriminant* of the quadratic equation found in step 1.\n\nRecall that:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}>0$, then the system of equations has $2$ distinct real solutions.\n- If $\\mathcal{D}=0$, then the system of equations has $1$ real solution.\n- If $\\mathcal{D}<0$, then the system of equations has no real solutions.\n\nThese results can also be viewed geometrically, an idea that we'll explore in future lessons.\n\nLet's take a look at an example where there are no real solutions to a nonlinear system."] [17592186069931 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of equations shown below.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 4 {y}^{2} \\\\ x = {y}^{2} + 12 y + 36\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, *both* equations are quadratic in the variable $y$. However, we can still solve this equation using substitution!\n\nFrom the second equation, we have\n\n$$\nx = y^2 + 12y + 36\n$$\n\nWe can substitute this into the first equation and solve the resulting quadratic in the variable $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 4y^{2} \\\\\ny^{2} + 12y + 36 &= 4y^{2} \\\\\n- 3y^{2} + 12y + 36 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides of the equation by $-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} - 4y - 12 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this factors as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 6)(y + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $y$ are $y_1 = -2$ and $y_2 = 6$.\n\nNow, we substitute each value of $y$ into the first equation to find the corresponding values of $x{:}$\n\n- For $y_1=-2$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =4{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4(-2)^{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $y_2=6$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =4{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4(6)^{2} \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to this system of equations are\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = (16,-2) \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad (x_2,y_2) = (144,6)\n$$\n\nSometimes, we need to solve one of the equations explicitly for one of the variables before we can perform the substitution. Let's see an example."] [17592186069976 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the points where the line $y=x-2$ intersects the radical function\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x+4}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph below, we can see that there is one point of intersection. Let's calculate its coordinates!\n\n![](images/a2/a22513e80fc1f369e4d29f9bb7c728a52ac3a74653e08907b407a8b4e06a6b82.png)\n\nAn intersection point is a common solution of the equations $y=x-2$ and\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x+4}\n$$\n\nIn other words, to get the coordinates of the intersection points, we have to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = \\sqrt{x + 4} \\\\ y = x - 2\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and then solving for $x$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x + 4} &= x - 2 \\\\\nx + 4 &= (x - 2)^{2} \\\\\nx + 4 &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we're dealing with square roots, we need to plug each of these solutions back into\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+4}=x-2\n$$\n\nto check whether it satisfies the original equation.\n\n- For $x=0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{0+4} & =0-2 \\\\ 2 & =-2,\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nso $x=0$ is not a solution of $\\sqrt{x+4}=x-2$.\n- For $x=5$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5+4} & =5-2 \\\\ 3 & =3,\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nso $x=5$ is indeed a solution of $\\sqrt{x+4}=x-2$.\n\nWe found that $x=5$ is the only solution of\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+4}=x-2\n$$\n\nWe now calculate the $y$-coordinate of the intersection by plugging $x=5$ in one of the two original curves. Let's choose the line, since it's simpler:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x - 2 \\\\\n&= 5 - 2 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point of intersection is $(5,3)$.\n\nLet's label this point in our sketch:\n\n![](images/15/1598c4bcd31fbe9b13124298ad623026afd04e2706566600389fc766c91acc06.png)"] [17592186070001 :tutorial/content "We wish to find the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\vert 5x + 7 \\vert = 3x +9\n$$\n\nFor this equation to be true, either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nTherefore, we have the following two cases:\n\n$$\n|5x+7| = 5x+7 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad |5x+7| = -(5x+7)\n$$\n\nWe now consider these two cases separately:\n\n- If $\\mid 5x +7|=5x +7$, we obtain the following equation:\n$5x +7 = 3x +9$\nWe now solve this equation using the usual methods:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+7 & =3x+9 \\\\ 2x+7 & =9 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x+7| & =3x+9 \\\\ |5(1)+7| & =3(1)+9 \\\\ |5+7| & =3+9 \\\\ |12| & =12 \\\\ 12 & =12\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=1$ satisfies the original equation, it's a valid solution.\n- If $\\mid 5x +7|=-(5x +7)$, we obtain the following equation:\n$-(5x +7)=3x +9$\nWe now solve this equation using the usual methods:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(5x+7) & =3x+9 \\\\ -5x-7 & =3x+9 \\\\ -7 & =8x+9 \\\\ -16 & =8x \\\\ -2 & =x \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x+7| & =3(-2)+9 \\\\ |5(-2)+7| & =3(-2)+9 \\\\ |-10+7| & =-6+9 \\\\ |-3| & =3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=-2$ satisfies the original equation, it's a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of our equation are $x=-2, \\, 1$.\n\n![](images/a8/a8982c526d460e2968b5e06871f74adc32da549faeffa89ac58a39c4d84730d5.png)"] [17592186073596 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle DEF$ are congruent by ASA since we have the following pairs of congruent angles and included sides:$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{DE}$$\\angle A \\cong \\angle D$$\\angle B \\cong \\angle E$\n- Statement II is false. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding sides must be congruent. In particular,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =EF \\\\ 2x-5 & =5 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5\\ne 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Statement III is true. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding sides must be congruent. In particular,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =DF \\\\ 3 & =4y-1 \\\\ 4y & =4 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."] [17592186073652 :question/worked-solution "Partitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ into a ratio $a:b$ means we must\n\n- divide the segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $1:3$ implies\n\n$$\nAC:CB = 1:3\n$$\n\nThis means\n\n- there are $1+3 = 4$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{4}AB$.\n\nNext, consider another point $X$ that is horizontal with $A$ and directly below $C$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n![](images/29/29b01b5cc8cca113a14c16f95626faadb70993bbb42fab7fd77f011ecf0de734.png)\n\nTo get from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" horizontally from $A$ to $X$ and then \"rise\" vertically from $X$ to $C$:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{C} & ={x}_{A}+{\\color{black}{\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{B}-{x}_{A})}} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4}(6-2) \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{C} & ={y}_{A}+{\\color{black}{\\frac{1}{4}({y}_{B}-{y}_{A})}} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4}(9-1) \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 3 \\right)\n$$"] [17592186073662 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠DAB &= m∠DAE + m∠EAB \\\\\n&= 21^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 66^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\triangle{ADB}$ and $\\triangle{ACB}$ are inscribed in the circle, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{ADB}=m\\angle{ACB}=90^\\circ\n$$\n\n- For $\\triangle{ADB}$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}ABD & ={90}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}DAB \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}-{66}^{∘} \\\\  & ={24}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $\\triangle{ACB}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}ABC & ={90}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}CAB \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠EBC &= m∠ABC - m∠ABD \\\\\n&= 45^{∘} - 24^{∘} \\\\\n&= 21^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186074936 :tutorial/content "![](images/8f/8f850ea0f0b0cdd54995561870cd8d896f290b9fd6b07de11ca0674c1a8a412b.png)\n\nOur goal is to prove the following:\n\n> $m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}$\n\nTo prove this theorem, we construct a ray from the point $H$ that's parallel to $\\overline{FG}$, and then apply the corresponding angles postulate and alternate interior angles theorem.\n\nWe complete the proof as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: We start by constructing a line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$ through the point $H$ and parallel to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG}$.![](images/ca/ca53ee7d8d2f6390e9aa8692bc0f1400aa78f84cf5afa0457b13f86f0921199e.png)\n- **Step 2**: Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$, we have that the corresponding angles $\\angle{IHJ}$ and $\\angle{F}$ are congruent by the *corresponding angles postulate*.Let's add this to our diagram.![](images/18/185672f4600e86a4e6e92e49d46cf966a46206128b4113ec4e070ff987cb5a7b.png)\n- **Step 3**: Since $\\angle{IHJ} \\cong \\angle{F}$ are congruent, we have\n$m\\angle{IHJ} = m\\angle{F}$\nby the *definition of congruent angles*.\n- **Step 4**: Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$ are parallel, we have that the alternate interior angles $\\angle{JHG}$ and $\\angle{G}$ are congruent by the *alternate interior angles theorem*.Let's add this to our diagram.![](images/f4/f441d13eb67dbbd6d301d479dd3ac0d6dd0ac79fd6fbc56c8fc90521f5c7cd24.png)\n- **Step 5**: Since $\\angle{JHG}$ and $\\angle{G}$ are congruent, we have\n$m\\angle{JHG} = m\\angle{G}$\nby the *definition of congruent angles*.\n- **Step 6**: In steps 3 and 5, we showed that $m\\angle{IHJ}=m\\angle{F}$ and $m\\angle{JHG}=m\\angle{G}$, respectively. So, by the *addition principle*, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}IHJ & =m\\mathrm{∠}F \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}IHJ+m\\mathrm{∠}JHG & =m\\mathrm{∠}F+m\\mathrm{∠}JHG \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}IHJ+m\\mathrm{∠}JHG & =m\\mathrm{∠}F+m\\mathrm{∠}G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Step 7**: Notice that $\\overline{HJ}$ is the common side of the angles $\\angle{IHJ}$ and $\\angle{JHG}$. Therefore, by the *angle addition postulate*, we have that\n$m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG}$.\n- **Step 8**: In steps 7 and 6, respectively, we showed that\n${\\color{blue}m\\angle{IHG}} = m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG}$,\nand\n$m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG} = {\\color{blue}m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}}$.\nTherefore, by the *transitive property of equality*, we get\n${\\color{blue}m\\angle{IHG}} = {\\color{blue}m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}}$,\nas required.\n\nAgain, it's **highly recommended** that you practice reconstructing this proof."] [17592186075008 :tutorial/content "We can partition any segment in a given ratio using techniques like those discussed for horizontal and vertical segments.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the point $C$ on the segment from the point $A(-1,0)$ to the point $B(7,4)$ that divides the segment into the ratio $1:1$.\n\n![](images/8e/8e67f8d03c2238fa342b98a58fd2527419f6f52ea98543eb85247d4c3706352a.png)\n\nPartitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ into a ratio $a:b$ means we must\n\n- divide the segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $1:1$ implies\n\n$$\nAC:CB = 1:1\n$$\n\nThis means\n\n- there are $1+1 = 2$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{2}AB$.\n\nNext, consider another point $X$ that is horizontal with $A$ and directly above $C$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n![](images/1e/1e2d40e0648ba95a734dd9166852907759c8f50af06653d7b1a34785e1944d6a.png)\n\nTo go from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" horizontally from $A$ to $X$ and then \"rise\" vertically from $X$ to $C$.\n\nSo, we compute the coordinates of $C$ as follows:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{C} & ={x}_{A}+{\\color{red}{\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{B}-{x}_{A})}} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}(7-(-1)) \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{8}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{C} & ={y}_{A}+{\\color{blue}{\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{B}-{y}_{A})}} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}(4-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 2 \\right)\n$$"] [17592186078234 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. $\\triangle QST$ and $\\triangle SQR$ are congruent by AAS since we have the following pairs of congruent angles and corresponding non-included sides:$\\overline{QS}$ is their common side,$\\angle R \\cong \\angle T$, $\\angle TQS \\cong \\angle RSQ$.\n- Statement II is true. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding sides must be congruent. In particular, $\\overline{QR} \\cong \\overline{TS}$.\n- Statement III is false. From the diagram, $\\angle{SQT} \\cong \\angle{QSR}$. Hence,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}RSQ & =m\\mathrm{∠}TQS \\\\ 6x-{7}^{∘} & ={35}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & ={42}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={7}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186078344 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate this limit by direct substitution. Substituting $x=1$ into the limit, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{|x-1|}{\\sqrt{x-1}} & =\\frac{|1-1|}{\\sqrt{1-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|0|}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nNotice, however, that we only require the right-sided limit, i.e., $x\\to1^{+}$. If $x > 1$, then\n\n$$\n\\mid x-1 \\mid = x-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write this limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  1^{+}}\\frac{\\mid x - 1 \\mid}{\\sqrt{x - 1}} &= \\lim_{x \\to  1^{+}}\\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{x - 1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDirect substitution into the above still gives an indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 0 0\n$$\n\nHowever, we can rationalize the expression and then evaluate the limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to  {1}^{+}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{x - 1}}} & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {1}^{+}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{x - 1}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{x - 1}}{\\sqrt{x - 1}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {1}^{+}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{( x - 1 ) \\sqrt{x - 1}}{x - 1}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {1}^{+}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{( x - 1 )} \\sqrt{x - 1}}{\\cancel{x - 1}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {1}^{+}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{x - 1}}{1}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {1}^{+}} \\sqrt{x - 1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\sqrt{1 - 1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\sqrt{0} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0\\end{array}}\n$$"] [17592186078346 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0^-} \\dfrac{\\mid x \\mid}{x}\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate this limit by direct substitution. Substituting $x=0$ into the limit, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{|x|}{x} & =\\frac{|0|}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nNotice, however, that we only require the left-sided limit, i.e., $x\\to0^{-}$. If $x < 0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mid x \\mid = -x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate this limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to  {0}^{-}} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{| x |}{x}} ) & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {0}^{-}} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{- x}{x}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {0}^{-}} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{- \\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {0}^{-}} ( - 1 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = - 1\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0^-} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2}}{x} = -1\n$$"] [17592186078349 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate this limit by direct substitution. Substituting $x=2$ into the limit, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{|{x}^{2}-4|}{x-2} & =\\frac{|{2}^{2}-4|}{2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4-4|}{2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nTo evaluate the limit, we factor the numerator and rewrite the limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x \\to 2^-} \\dfrac {\\vert x^2 - 4 \\vert} {x - 2} &=\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} \\dfrac {\\vert (x+2)(x-2) \\vert} {x - 2} \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} \\dfrac {\\vert x+2\\vert \\cdot \\vert x-2 \\vert} {x - 2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $x<2$, we have\n\n- $\\mid x+2 \\mid = x+2$ and\n- $\\mid x-2 \\mid = -(x-2)$.\n\nSubstituting the above into our limit and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to  {2}^{-}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{| x + 2 | \\cdot  | x - 2 |}{x - 2}} & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {2}^{-}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{- ( x + 2 ) ( x - 2 )}{x - 2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {2}^{-}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{- ( x + 2 ) \\cancel{( x - 2 )}}{\\cancel{x - 2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {2}^{-}} - ( x + 2 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = - ( 2 + 2 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = - 4.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} \\dfrac {\\vert x^2 - 4 \\vert} {x - 2} = -4\n$$"] [17592186082910 :question/worked-solution "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the convergent geometric series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2^n}{3^n} = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n$$\n\nWe know the above series converges because it is a geometric series with common ratio\n\n$$\n\\mid r \\mid = \\dfrac{2}{3} < 1\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{3(2^n)+1}{2(3^n)+1}, \\qquad b_n = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {a}_{n}}{ {b}_{n}}} & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 ( {2}^{n} ) + 1}{2 ( {3}^{n} ) + 1}} \\div  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {2}^{n}}{ {3}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 ( {2}^{n} ) + 1}{2 ( {3}^{n} ) + 1}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {3}^{n}}{ {2}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {2}^{n} ( 3 + {2}^{- n} )}{ {3}^{n} ( 2 + {3}^{- n} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {3}^{n}}{ {2}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {2}^{n}} ( 3 + {2}^{- n} )}{\\cancel{ {3}^{n}} ( 2 + {3}^{- n} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {3}^{n}}}{\\cancel{ {2}^{n}}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 + {2}^{- n}}{2 + {3}^{- n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 + 2 ( 0 )}{2 + 3 ( 0 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nconverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nconverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{3(2^n)+1}{2(3^n)+1}\n$$\n\nconverges."] [17592186082913 :question/worked-solution "From the graph of the function, we note the following:\n\n- The function has only one vertical asymptote, $x = 1$. Therefore, the function's denominator must be zero at $x = 1$, but the numerator should not be zero at this point. This allows us to discard the following option:\n$y = \\dfrac{2}{x+1}$,\n- The function has no $x$-intercept, and so we must have $f(x) \\neq 0$ for all $x$. This allows us to discard the following option:\n$y = \\dfrac{x-3}{x-1}$\nSo, we're left with the following options:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\dfrac{3}{1-x}, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{2x^2 + 3}{(x-1)^2}\n$$\n- The horizontal asymptote is $y = 2$. This means that $y \\to 2$ as $\\mid x \\mid \\to\\infty$. Note that for large $\\mid x \\mid$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{1-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{x}-1)} \\\\  & \\approx 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, we can discard the following option:\n$y = \\dfrac{3}{1-x}$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the only one that satisfies the above conditions is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{2x^2 + 3}{(x-1)^2}\n$$"] [17592186083049 :question/worked-solution "We can write the summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(200)(3n + 2) &= ∑_(n = 1)^(200)3n + ∑_(n = 1)^(200)2 \\\\\n&= 3∑_(n = 1)^(200)n + 2∑_(n = 1)^(200)1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the two series separately.\n\n- For the first series, we use the formula\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^N n = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1)\n$$\nwith $N=200$, and we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{200}n & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 200\\cdot 201) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 20\\hspace{0.167em}100 \\\\  & =60\\hspace{0.167em}300.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- In the second series, $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^{200} 1}\n$$ is just $1$ added together $200$ times, which is $200 \\cdot 1 = 200$. So, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2\\sum_{n=1}^{200} 1 =2 \\cdot 200 = 400.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{200} (3n+2) = 60\\,300 + 400 = 60\\,700\n$$"] [17592186083050 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(50)(3n^{2} + 2n) &= ∑_(n = 1)^(50)3n^{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(50)2n \\\\\n&= 3∑_(n = 1)^(50)n^{2} + 2∑_(n = 1)^(50)n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate the two series using our formulas.\n\n- For the first series, we use the formula\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^N n^2 = \\dfrac1 6 N (N+1)(2N+1)\n$$\nwith $N=50$, and we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{n}^{2} & =3(\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 50(50+1)(2\\cdot 50+1)) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 50\\cdot 51\\cdot 101 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 42\\hspace{0.167em}925 \\\\  & =128\\hspace{0.167em}775.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the second series, we use the formula\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^N n = \\dfrac1 2 N (N+1)\n$$\nwith $N=50$, and we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}n & =2(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 50\\cdot (50+1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\hspace{0.167em}275 \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}550.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(50)(3n^{2} + 2n) &= 128775 + 2550 \\\\\n&= 131325\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186083053 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(50)(3n^{3} + 2n) &= ∑_(n = 1)^(50)3n^{3} + ∑_(n = 1)^(50)2n \\\\\n&= 3∑_(n = 1)^(50)n^{3} + 2∑_(n = 1)^(50)n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate the two series using our formulas.\n\n- For the first series, we use the formula\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^N n^3 = \\dfrac1 4 N^2 (N+1)^2\n$$\nwith $N=50$, and we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{n}^{3} & =3(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {50}^{2}(50+1)^{2}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {50}^{2}\\cdot {51}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\hspace{0.167em}625\\hspace{0.167em}625 \\\\  & =4\\hspace{0.167em}876\\hspace{0.167em}875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the second series, we use the formula\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^N n = \\dfrac1 2 N (N+1)\n$$\nwith $N=50$, and we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}n & =2(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 50(50+1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\hspace{0.167em}275 \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}550.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(50)(3n^{3} + 2n) &= 4876875 + 2550 \\\\\n&= 4879425\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186083089 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each series in-turn.\n\n- Let $a_n = \\dfrac{2(n-1)!}{n!}$. Simplifying gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2(n-1)!}{n!} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(n-1)!}{(n-1)!\\cdot n} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(n-1)!}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(n-1)!}\\cdot n} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand from here we see that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} a_n = \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{2}{n} = 0.\n$$\nSo the $n$th term test gives no conclusion as to the convergence or divergence of $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2(n-1)!}{n!}.\n$$\n- Next, let $b_n = \\dfrac{e^n}{3n+1}$. We have that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} b_n = \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{e^n}{3n+1} = \\infty\n$$\nbecause the numerator grows much faster than the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{e^n}{3n+1}\n$$ is divergent.\n- Finally, let $c_n = \\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^n$. This is a geometric sequence with $\\mid r \\mid = \\dfrac 3 2 > 1$. So, we have that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} c_n = \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^n = \\infty,\n$$\nand by the $n$th term test, $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^n\n$$ is divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, the $n$th term test guarantees that II and III diverge, but gives no conclusion regarding the convergence or divergence of I."] [17592186083608 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 x + y = 4 \\\\ \\displaystyle 3 x - 2 y = 13\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe variable $y$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1. Multiply the first equation by $2$ and obtain\n$\\begin{aligned}4x+2y & =8.\\end{aligned}$.\n2. Add the multiplied equation to the second equation to get\n\n{{field-1}}\n$= 21$."] [17592186083609 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + 4 y = 2 \\\\ x + 3 y = - 1\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe variable $x$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1. Multiply the second equation by $-3$ and obtain\n$\\begin{aligned}-3x-9y & =3.\\end{aligned}$.\n2. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n\n{{field-1}}\n$= 5$."] [17592186083779 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 v - 10 w = 4 \\\\ \\displaystyle - v + 6 w = 7\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe variable $v$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1. Multiply the second equation by $2$ and obtain\n$\\begin{aligned}-2v+12w & =14.\\end{aligned}$.\n2. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n$⋯?⋯ = 18$.\n\nWhat's the missing expression?"] [17592186083780 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}12 v - 32 w = - 8 \\\\ - 2 v + 6 w = 2\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe variable $v$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1. Multiply the second equation by $6$ and obtain\n$\\begin{aligned}-12v+36w & =12.\\end{aligned}$.\n2. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n$⋯?⋯ = 4$.\n\nFind the missing expression, and hence find the solution $(v, w)$ of the system."] [17592186084504 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to identify a *possible* equation of a rational function from its graph.\n\nConsider the rational function $y=f(x)$ below. What can we figure out about $f(x)$ based on its graph?\n\n![](images/f8/f89d70391ba43423e3600ab8a5eff6421ee4828da82e36239334116c5af76fc9.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we deduce the following:\n\n- The function has a vertical asymptote at $x = -1$. Therefore, the function's denominator must be zero at $x = -1$, but the numerator should *not* be zero at this point. So we're expecting a factor like $(x+1)$ in the denominator:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{?}{x+1}\n$$\n\n- The function has an $x$-intercept at $x = -2$. Therefore, the function's numerator must be zero at $x = -2$, but the denominator should *not* be zero at this point. So we're expecting a factor like $(x+2)$ in the numerator. This suggests that the function $f(x)$ could be given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x+2}{x+1}\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, this function does indeed give the above graph. However, to be sure, let's make a few additional checks:\n\n- The $y$-intercept shown on the graph is $(0,2)$, and so we must have $f(0) = 2$. This is indeed true of $f(x)$, since\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0+2}{0+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The horizontal asymptote shown on the graph is $y=1$. This means that $y\\to 1$ as $\\mid x \\mid \\to\\infty$. This is indeed true of $f(x)$, since for large $\\mid x \\mid$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+\\frac{2}{x})}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1+0}{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x+2}{x+1}\n$$\n\ncould be the function shown in the graph."] [17592186087559 :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the $x$-intercepts, we set $y=0$ and solve for $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= (t - 2)(3t - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side equals zero if one of the factors is zero.\n\n- For the factor $(t-2)$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the factor $(3t-1)$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3t-1) & =0 \\\\ 3t & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our solutions for $t$ into the equation\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac 1t\n$$\n\n- Substituting $t=2$ gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Substituting $t=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},0\\right)\n$$\n\nand $(3,0)$."] [17592186087619 :question/worked-solution "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2\\sqrt n}.\n$$ Rewriting this series gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\sqrt{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\cdot {n}^{1/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\dfrac 5 2$. Since $p > 1$, the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n- For the second series, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{n^2} = 5\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{{n^2}},\n$$\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=2$. Since $p>1$, the series is convergent by the $p$-series test.\n- For the third series, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{{n}^{1/2}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{1}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=1$ (the harmonic series). Since $p \\leq 1$, the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\nTherefore, only series I and II are convergent."] [17592186087646 :question/worked-solution "To find the greatest common factor of two numbers, we first find the prime factorization of both numbers.\n\n- Since $3$ is a prime number, we immediately obtain the prime decomposition:\n\n$$\n3^3 = 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3\n$$\n\n- Since $21= 3\\cdot 7$, the prime decomposition $21^2$ can be obtained as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{21}^{2} & =21\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 7)\\cdot (3\\cdot 7) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3\\cdot 7\\cdot 7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the decompositions side-by-side, we pair off the factors as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{3} &= 3_(―) \\cdot 3_(―) \\cdot 3 \\\\\n21^{2} &= 3_(―) \\cdot 3_(―) \\cdot 7 \\cdot 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(3^{3}, 21^{2}) = 3^{2}\n$$"] [17592186088922 :tutorial/content "Consider the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} = 1\n$$\n\nand line $y = 2x$, sketched below. We can see from the diagram that the line and the ellipse intersect at $(2,4)$ and $(-2,-4)$.\n\n![](images/b0/b0a3676e298879fb74c0488ded0ad6fd469592eeba21f4d96dc8e5555886b641.png)\n\nBut how can we determine this mathematically, without relying on the graph?\n\nTo find the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we can substitute $y = 2x$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + \\frac{y^{2}}{24} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + ((2x)^{2})/(24) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + \\frac{4x^{2}}{24} &= 1 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 4x^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n6x^{2} &= 24 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4 \\\\\nx &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, to find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we can substitute $x = \\pm 2$ into the equation of the line and compute the $y$-values.\n\n- Substituting $x = -2$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\ y & =2\\cdot (-2) \\\\ y & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis tells us that one intersection point is $(-2, -4)$.\n- Substituting $x = 2$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\ y & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis tells us that the other intersection point is $(2, 4)$.\n\nSo we find that the two intersection points are $(-2,-4)$ and $(2,4)$, which matches up perfectly with what we see in the graph."] [17592186092147 :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume of a pyramid using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3}Ah\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is the area of the base and $h$ is the altitude of the pyramid.\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- We are told that $\\overline{SH}$ is the altitude of the pyramid. So, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle h=SH=8\\,\\textrm{in}.\n$$\n- The line segment $\\overline{CL}$ is the height of $\\triangle{BCD}$ corresponding to the side $\\overline{BD}$. So, we compute the area of $\\triangle{BCD}$ as\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{BD\\cdot CL}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\text{in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nA= \\dfrac{63}{2}\\,\\textrm{in}^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nh=8\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we get\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\left(\\dfrac{63}{2}\\right) (8) = 84 \\: \\text{in}^3\n$$"] [17592186096405 :question/worked-solution "We want to find the best model for the scatterplot data shown, in the quadratic form\n\n$$\ny = ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwhere $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants, with\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- The data in the scatterplot appear to form a downward-opening parabola. Therefore, the leading coefficient $a$ must be negative.\n- The points look symmetric about the line $x=4$. Using the axis of symmetry formula $x=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}$ and solving for $b$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\ 4 & =-\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\ b & =-8a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $a$ is negative, the coefficient of the linear term should be positive.\n- The $y$-intercept of the quadratic model is near $(0,650)$.\n\nTherefore, of the given choices, only choice\n\n$$\ny = -2.28x^{2} + 20.2x+652.76\n$$\n\nis the best quadratic model for the data in the scatterplot."] [17592186096539 :question/worked-solution "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since there were $25$ total students, and only $5$ of them passed both tests, we have that\n$$\n\\displaystyle P(A)=\\dfrac{5}{25}=0.2.\n$$\n- Statement II is true. The complement $A'$ is the opposite of the event $A$, \"the student passed both tests.\" So $A'$ is the event \"the student failed at least one test.\"\n- Statement III is true. Since we know that $P(A) = 0.2$, we can calculate $P\\left(A'\\right)$ using the formula\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ P({A}^{'}) & =1-P(A) \\\\  & =1-0.2 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186096557 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence $a_n$ is defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $a_n$ is increasing or decreasing, we can use the following test:\n\n- If a sequence $a_n$ is *increasing,* then for all $n\\geq 1$, we have\n$a_{n+1} \\geq a_{n} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad a_{n+1} - a_n \\geq 0$.\n- If a sequence $a_n$ is *decreasing,* then for all $n\\geq 1$, we have\n$a_{n+1} \\leq a_{n} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad a_{n+1} - a_n \\leq 0$.\n- If neither of these conditions holds, the sequence is *neither increasing nor decreasing*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the difference $a_{n+1} - a_n$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n} & =3-2(n+1)-(n+1)^{2}-(3-2n-{n}^{2}) \\\\  & =3-2n-2-({n}^{2}+2n+1)-3+2n+{n}^{2} \\\\  & =3-\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{2n}-2-\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{n}^{2}}-2n-1-3+\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{2n}+\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =-3-2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich is *negative* for all $n\\geq 1$. To see this concretely, set $b_n = -3-2n$. Computing the first few terms of $b_n$ we get\n$-5, \\quad -7, \\quad -9, \\quad -11, \\quad \\ldots\\,$.\n\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $a_{n+1} - a_n \\leq 0$ for all $n\\geq 1$, we can conclude that $a_n$ is a decreasing sequence.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186096600 :question/worked-solution "The total surface area $(S)$ of a pyramid equals the sum of the lateral surface area $(S_L)$ and the base area $(S_B)$ of the pyramid:\n\n$$\nS = S_L + S_B\n$$\n\nWith this in mind, let's find $S_L$ and $S_B$ in turn.\n\n- We can find the lateral surface area of a regular pyramid using the formula\n$S_L = \\dfrac{1}{2} p l$,\nwhere $p$ is the base's perimeter and $l$ is the pyramid's slant height.Given the side $s = 7 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ of the regular (equilateral) triangular base, its perimeter is equal to\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =3s=3(7)=21\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{yd}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, substituting $p = 21 \\, \\textrm{yd}$ and $l = 12 \\, \\textrm{yd}$ into the formula, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_L = \\dfrac{1}{2}(21)(12) = 126 \\, \\textrm{yd}^2.\n$$\n- The base of the triangular pyramid is an equilateral triangle. In our cases, it's a triangle with side length $s = 7 \\, \\textrm{yd}$. So, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_B = \\dfrac{s^2\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\dfrac{7^2\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\dfrac{49\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\, \\textrm{yd}^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total surface area of the pyramid is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= S_{L} + S_{B} \\\\\n&= (126 + \\frac{49\\sqrt{3}}{4})yd^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186097856 :tutorial/content "A line may intersect a parabola at one or two points. It is also possible that there is no point of intersection.\n\nIn the figure below, we see that the parabola\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2 =y-1\n$$\n\nintersects the line $y = 2x - 1$ at two places, $A$ and $B$. Let's compute the coordinates of these points.\n\n![](images/23/2312bbdd5cecf1c3523acfb5a6f8fc83eaf73334966b4f946d46b22facfb3162.png)\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we substitute $y = 2x - 1$ into the equation of the parabola and solve the resulting equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 1)^{2} &= y - 1 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= (2x - 1) - 1 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 2x - 2 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x + 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(x - 3) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 1, 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection, we substitute each of these values of $x$ into the equation of the line.\n\n- For $x=1$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-1 \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the coordinates of the first point of intersection are $(1,1)$.\n- Now, for $x=3$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-1 \\\\  & =2(3)-1 \\\\  & =6-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the coordinates of the second point of intersection are $(3,5)$.\n\nTherefore, the parabola\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2 =y-1\n$$\n\nintersects the line $y=2x-1$ at the points $(1,1)$ and $(3,5)$."] [17592186100775 :question/worked-solution "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let\n$a_n= \\dfrac{e^{2n}}{n^{99}}$.\nThen, we have $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}a_n=\\infty,\n$$ since the exponential term in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{e^{2n}}{n^{99}}\n$$\nis divergent.\n- For the second series, we use the comparison test. Observe that\n$\\ln (2n + 1) > 1⟹(\\ln (2n + 1))/(n) > \\frac{1}{n}$\nfor all $n\\geq 1$. Now, the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, by the comparison test, the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{\\ln(2n+1)}{n}\n$$\nis also divergent.\n- For the final series, we use the ratio test. Let\n$a_n= \\dfrac{4^n}{n!}$.\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}(\\frac{{4}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}÷\\frac{{4}^{n}}{n!}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}(\\frac{{4}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{{4}^{n}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}(\\frac{4({4}^{n})}{n!(n+1)}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{{4}^{n}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}(\\frac{4\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{({4}^{n})}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{n!}(n+1)}\\cdot \\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{n!}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{4}^{n}}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\left(\\frac{4}{n+1}\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $L=0 < 1$, we conclude that the series is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series III converges."] [17592186100798 :question/worked-solution "For any convergent series\n\n$$\nS = \\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nthe remainder (or error) $R_N$ obtained when $S$ is approximated using its $N$th partial sum $s_N$ is defined as\n\n$$\nR_N = S - s_N\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions for the integral test, then the remainder is bounded by\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\int_{N+1}^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{lower bound}} \\leq R_N \\leq \\underbrace{\\int_{N}^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{upper bound}}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_n = f(n)$ for\n\n$$\nn = 1, 2, 3, \\ldots\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5^x}\n$$\n\nis positive, continuous, and decreasing for\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions for the integral test.\n\nWe wish to compute an *upper* bound for the remainder when $S$ is approximated by $s_{4}$. Therefore, $N = 4$, and we have\n\n$$\nR_{4} \\leq \\int_{4}^\\infty f(x) \\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet's now calculate the upper bound:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4} & \\le {\\int }_{4}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{5}^{-x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}5}\\hspace{0.167em}|}_{4}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}5}\\lim_{b\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{[\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}]}_{4}^{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}5}\\lim_{b\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}(\\frac{1}{{5}^{b}}-\\frac{1}{{5}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}5}(0-\\frac{1}{625}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}5}\\left(-\\frac{1}{625}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{625\\operatorname{ln}5} \\\\  & \\approx 0.000\\hspace{0.167em}994\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places."] [17592186100799 :question/worked-solution "For any convergent series\n\n$$\nS=\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nthe remainder (or error) $R_N$ obtained when $S$ is approximated using its $N$th partial sum $s_N$ is defined as\n\n$$\nR_N = S - s_N\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions for the integral test, then the remainder is bounded by\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\int_{N+1}^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{lower bound}} \\leq R_N \\leq \\underbrace{\\int_{N}^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{upper bound}}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_n = f(n)$ for\n\n$$\nn =1, 2, 3, \\ldots\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)^3}\n$$\n\nis positive, continuous, and decreasing for\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions for the integral test.\n\nWe wish to compute a *lower* bound for the remainder when $S$ is approximated by $s_{8}$. Therefore, $N = 8$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{8 + 1}^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int_{9}^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x \\leq R_{8}\n$$\n\nLet's now calculate the lower bound:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{8} & \\ge {\\int }_{9}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{9}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}(2x+1)^{-3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}|}_{9}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\lim_{b\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{[\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}]}_{9}^{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\lim_{b\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}(\\frac{1}{(2b+1)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 9+1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(0-\\frac{1}{361}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\left(-\\frac{1}{361}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\hspace{0.167em}444}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186100800 :question/worked-solution "For any convergent series\n\n$$\nS=\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nthe remainder (or error) $R_N$ obtained when $S$ is approximated using its $N$th partial sum $s_N$ is defined as\n\n$$\nR_N = S - s_N\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions for the integral test, then the remainder is bounded by\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\int_{N+1}^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{lower bound}} \\leq R_N \\leq \\underbrace{\\int_{N}^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{upper bound}}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_n = f(n)$ for\n\n$$\nn=1,2,3\\ldots\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{1}{e^x}\n$$\n\nis positive, continuous, and decreasing for\n\n$$\nx\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions for the integral test.\n\nWe wish to compute a *upper* bound for the remainder when $S$ is approximated by $s_{11}$. Therefore, $N=11$, and we have\n\n$$\nR_{11} \\leq \\int_{11}^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet's now calculate the upper bound:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{11} & \\le {\\int }_{11}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{11}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{e}^{-x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}|}_{11}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{b\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{[\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}]}_{11}^{b} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{b\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}(\\frac{1}{{e}^{b}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{11}}) \\\\  & =-(0-\\frac{1}{{e}^{11}}) \\\\  & =-\\left(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{11}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{11}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can form an upper bound for $S$ by adding $s_{11}$ to both sides of our inequality, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}R_{11} & \\le \\frac{1}{e^{11}} \\\\ s_{11} + R_{11} & \\le s_{11} + \\frac{1}{e^{11}} \\\\ S & \\le s_{11} + \\frac{1}{e^{11}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186101224 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=7$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)} & ={\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}|}_{x=7} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(7)}{{7}^{2}\\cdot g(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{49\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x))^{′}}{({x}^{2}g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 7}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=7$ again, and this time, we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 7}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=7} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(7)}{2\\cdot 7\\cdot g(7)+{7}^{2}\\cdot {g}^{′}(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+49\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{98}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}=\\frac{1}{98}.\n$$"] [17592186101225 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=10$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)} & ={\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)}|}_{x=10} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(10)+5}{20+g(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5+5}{20+(-20)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 10}\\frac{(f(x)+5)^{′}}{(2x+g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=10$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=10} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(10)}{2+{g}^{′}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2+(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 10}\\frac{({f}^{′}(x))^{′}}{(2+{g}^{′}(x))^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=10,$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)}|}_{x=10} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{″}(10)}{{g}^{″}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{-6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186105670 :question/worked-solution "We will first compute the number of ways of selecting $8$ chocolates. Then, we will compute the number of ways of selecting $4$ nougats. Finally, we will apply the multiplication principle to compute the total number of combinations that can be obtained.\n\n- The problem of selecting $8$ chocolates is analogous to placing $r$ identical objects into $n$ categories. We are choosing $r=8$ chocolates from $n=4$ categories. The number of ways to place $r$ identical objects into $n$ categories is given by the formula\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r + n - 1}{r} \\right) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( r + n - 1 ) !}{r ! ( n - 1 ) !}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\nSo, the number of ways to choose $r=8$ chocolates from $n=4$ categories is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(8+4-1)!}{8!(4-1)!} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11!}{8!\\cdot (11-8)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{11!}{8!\\cdot 3!} \\\\  & =165.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The number of ways of choosing $4$ nougats from $5$ possible options without repetition is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4}) & =\\frac{5!}{4!\\cdot (5-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{4!\\cdot 1!} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the multiplication principle, we conclude that we can obtain\n\n$$\n165\\cdot 5 =825\n$$\n\ncombinations in total."] [17592186105682 :question/worked-solution "If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, then the quadrilateral is a parallelogram. Thus, $ABCD$ is a parallelogram if $AO=CO$ and $BO=DO$.\n\n- Using the first equality, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AO & =CO \\\\ 4x+3 & =y+2 \\\\ y & =4x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Using the second equality, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BO & =DO \\\\ 5-x & =y-1 \\\\ x & =6-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ny = 4x+1\n$$\n\nfrom the first equation into the above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 6 - y \\\\\nx &= 6 - (4x + 1) \\\\\nx &= 5 - 4x \\\\\n5x &= 5 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x$ back into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4x + 1 \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 4 + 1 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ and $y=5$."] [17592186107002 :tutorial/content "![](images/a9/a9a087086d2653ff95b039c1a05ea0dcf310df72243cfee248c8544f179adf37.png)\n\nConsider the parallelogram $ABCD$ above, where\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = 70^\\circ\n$$\n\nLet's find the measures of the other angles.\n\n- First, we know that opposite angles in a parallelogram are congruent. Therefore, $m\\angle C = 70^\\circ$.![](images/3a/3a32408673bb4a42be19f48323476339ed7aaaf6c91243b29d8fc4fcb9f0818a.png)\n\n- Next, we know that consecutive angles in a parallelogram are supplementary. Therefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}B & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}A \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{70}^{∘} \\\\  & ={110}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nLet's add this to our diagram.![](images/f9/f92d9f2354b52d90074aaca802937468645749d09aa314f0fa915947ee3d4682.png)\n\n- Finally, we apply the fact that opposite angles are congruent to conclude that $m\\angle D = 110^\\circ$.![](images/d9/d91adf574a75ce2095129724e5eb241e0aebb6f0b3c8182c41972c36e5f876ef.png)"] [17592186107110 :tutorial/content "Here, we derive the three useful triangle formulas that we have used throughout the lesson.\n\n- For an equilateral triangle with side length $a$, the area $A$ is given by the formula\n$A = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}a^2$.\nTo see why this is true, consider the equilateral triangle below.![](images/85/85b54af73ee6951c83aa675e7e34c6403c91d3f8d11ca198c1a4f91510e8d976.png)\nFor this triangle, we see that we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{base} & =a, \\\\ \\text{height} & =a\\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstituting this into the formula for the area of a triangle, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\dfrac 1 2 \\cdot a \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}a = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}a^2.\n$$\n\n![](images/20/20b7814be68e26f3e4b0426f6726d571a2dc0c112705dc6cad178bf0042d23a3.png)\n\n- For an isosceles right triangle with leg $b$ and hypotenuse $c$, the area $A$ of the triangle is\n$A = \\dfrac12 b^2$.\nThis is a straightforward application of the formula for the area of a triangle. Clearly, we have $\\textrm{base} = \\textrm{height} = b$, and therefore, the formula follows immediately.We also have the formula\n$A = \\dfrac14 c^2$.\nTo see why this is true, recall that, for an isosceles right triangle, the base $b$ and hypotenuse $c$ are related by the equation $c = \\sqrt 2 b$. Now, substituting for $b$ into the formula $A=\\dfrac12 b^2$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\dfrac12\\left(\\dfrac{c}{\\sqrt 2}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac14 c^2.\n$$"] [17592186110424 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle DEF$ are congruent by the SAS criterion since we have the following pairs of congruent sides and included angles:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}BA^(―) & ≅ED^(―) \\\\ AC^(―) & ≅DF^(―) \\\\ ∠A & ≅∠D\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Statement II is false. Since the two triangles are congruent, the corresponding angles must be congruent too. Therefore, we obtain\n$\\angle{B} \\cong \\angle{E}$.\n- Statement III is true. Since the two triangles are congruent, the corresponding sides must be congruent too. Hence, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =EF \\\\ 2x & =x+2 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186110428 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle DEF$ are congruent by HL (hypotenuse-leg) since we have the following congruent legs, hypotenuses, and right angles:$m\\angle{B}=m\\angle{E}=90^\\circ$$\\overline{AB}\\cong \\overline{ED}$$\\overline{AC}\\cong \\overline{DF}$\n- Statement II is true. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding angles must be congruent. In particular,\n$\\angle C \\cong \\angle F$\n- Statement III is true. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding sides must be congruent. In particular, $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{EF}$. Thus,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =EF \\\\ 3y-8 & =2y-4 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186110544 :question/worked-solution "For each row/column in a joint frequency table:\n\n- the sum of the joint frequencies equals the marginal frequency (or total) for that row/column, and\n- the sum of the marginal frequencies in the totals row/column equals the total $(85)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each row/column:\n\n- Looking at the first row, there are $44$ boys in total.\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">German</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Spanish</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">French</th>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Boys</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$44$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Girls</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$z$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals </th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$t$ </td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$w$ </td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$29$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$85$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8+x+14 & =44 \\\\ 22+x & =44 \\\\ x & =44-22 \\\\ x & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe add this value to our table.\n- Looking at the third column, we see that there are $29$ children who prefer to learn French.\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">German</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Spanish</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">French</th>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Boys</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$44$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Girls</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$z$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals </th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$t$ </td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$w$ </td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$29$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$85$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14+y & =29 \\\\ y & =29-14 \\\\ y & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe add this value to our table.\n\nBy similar reasoning, we can deduce the following:\n\n- Considering the number of children who prefer to learn German, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =8+12=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Considering the number of children who prefer to learn Spanish, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =22+14=36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Since there are $85$ children in total, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}44+z & =85 \\\\ z & =85-44 \\\\ z & =41.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur completed frequency table is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">German</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Spanish</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">French</th>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Boys</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$44$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Girls</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}15}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}41}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}36}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$29$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$85$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nw + y + z = 36 + 15 + 41 = 92\n$$"] [17592186115045 :question/worked-solution "The logarithms on the left-hand side share the same base, so we can combine them using the product rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{2} x + \\log_{2} (x + 3) &= 2 \\\\\n\\log_{2} [x(x + 3)] &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the equation for $x$ using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x + 3) &= 2^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} + 3x &= 4 \\\\\nx^{2} + 3x - 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 4)(x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x = -4$ or $x = 1$.\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting them back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(1)+{log}_{2}(1+3) & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ {log}_{2}(1)+{log}_{2}(4) & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ {log}_{2}(1\\cdot 4) & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ {log}_{2}(4) & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ {log}_{2}({2}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution.\n- Substituting $x=-4$ back into the original equation, we get\n$\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(-4)+{log}_{2}(-4+3) & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ {log}_{2}(-4)+{log}_{2}(-1) & \\ne 2\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\mathbf{\\times }}}\\end{aligned}$\nbecause $\\log_2 (-4)$ and $\\log_2(-1)$ are undefined. Therefore, $x=-4$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $x=1$."] [17592186115769 :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous on $[- π, π]$?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}(s) & =2s\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{s}{\\operatorname{sin}s}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2{s}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\mathbf{q}(s) & =3\\operatorname{cos}s\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}(2s)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{s}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}(s) & =\\operatorname{cot}(s+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}(2s)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{cos}(2s)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115773 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t \\to  1}[f(t) \\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}\\pi t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}(t-1)}{t-1},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2-t},\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115776 :question/problem "Find the value of $(3h(t) - 2g(t))^{′}$ at $t = 2$, if\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{h}(t) & =⟨2{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}t-3,\\hspace{0.222em}8+{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}+2t,\\hspace{0.222em}8,\\hspace{0.222em}2{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115803 :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $f(t) = u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable, given that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}(t-1) \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{sin}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\sqrt{1+t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119508 :question/worked-solution "To determine if the relationship is linear or exponential, we need to determine how $V$ changes as $t$ increases at each step:\n\n- For a linear relationship, there should be a common *difference* between successive values of $V$.\n- For an exponential relationship, there should be a common *ratio* between successive values of $V$.\n\nLet's check whether the table shows a linear or exponential relationship with that in mind.\n\n- First, let's check for a common ratio by dividing successive values of $V{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{V(2)}{V(1)} & =\\frac{859}{687}\\approx 1.25 \\\\ \\frac{V(3)}{V(2)} & =\\frac{1\\hspace{0.167em}074}{859}\\approx 1.25 \\\\ \\frac{V(4)}{V(3)} & =\\frac{1\\hspace{0.167em}343}{1\\hspace{0.167em}074}\\approx 1.25 \\\\ \\frac{V(5)}{V(4)} & =\\frac{1\\hspace{0.167em}679}{1\\hspace{0.167em}343}\\approx 1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis list of quotients shows a definite (although approximate) common ratio.\n- Now, let's check for a linear pattern by finding the difference between successive values of $V{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2)-V(1) & =859-687=172 \\\\ V(3)-V(2) & =1\\hspace{0.167em}074-859=215 \\\\ V(4)-V(3) & =1\\hspace{0.167em}343-1\\hspace{0.167em}074=269 \\\\ V(5)-V(4) & =1\\hspace{0.167em}679-1\\hspace{0.167em}343=336\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis list of differences does not show a definite common difference.\n\nTherefore, the function that best models the relationship is an exponential function with a common ratio of approximately $1.25$."] [17592186119728 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following rules regarding the products of even and odd functions:\n\n$$\neven \\cdot even = even; odd \\cdot odd = even; even \\cdot odd = odd; odd \\cdot even = odd\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $f(x) = x^3-x^5$. Then, $f(x)$ is odd since\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nfor all $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x) = \\sin^2 x$. Then, $g(x)$ is even since\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={sin}^{2}(-x) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}x \\\\  & =g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nfor all $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- Statement III is true. Notice that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\cdot {sin}^{2}x & =f(x)\\cdot g(x) \\\\  & =\\text{odd}\\cdot \\text{even} \\\\  & =\\text{odd}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186119733 :question/worked-solution "Below is an example of a tetrahedron.\n\n![](images/ad/adcc202ad86b782fd153566d6454269dcd50e8f1d9aca37bc42d2266c142ced8.png)\n\nLet's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. A tetrahedron is a regular polyhedron that consists of $4$ congruent faces, each in the form of an equilateral triangle, and exactly $3$ faces meet at each vertex.\n- Statement II is true. Since there are $F=4$ faces, each in the form of a triangle, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = 4 \\cdot 3 = 12\n$$\nsides combined. Now, each edge of the polyhedron is created when two face sides coincide. So, the number of edges of the tetrahedron must be equal to\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{S}{2} = \\dfrac{12}{2} = 6.\n$$\n- Statement III is true. Substituting the number of edges $E=6$ and the number of faces $F=4$ into Euler's formula, we can solve for the number of vertices $V$ as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V-E+F & =2 \\\\ V-(6)+(4) & =2 \\\\ V-2 & =2 \\\\ V & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186124430 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\na_n=\\lambda^n\n$$\n\nThen, we have that\n\n$$\na_{n-1} = \\lambda^{n-1}, \\qquad a_{n-2} = \\lambda^{n-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our relation gives\n\n$$\n\\lambda^n = 8\\lambda^{n-1} - 16\\lambda^{n-2}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{n} - 8λ^{n - 1} + 16λ^{n - 2} &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{n - 2}(λ^{2} - 8λ + 16) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 8\\lambda + 16 = 0\n$$\n\nFactoring, we get\n\n$$\n(\\lambda - 4)^2 = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda = 4$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo, the general solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = A \\cdot 4^n + Bn \\cdot 4^n\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions:\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = 1$ into the general solution gives\n$A = 1$.\n- Substituting $a_1 = -8$ into the general solution gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =4A+4B \\\\ -2 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the fact that $A=1$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =1+B \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 1 \\cdot 4^{n} - 3n \\cdot 4^{n} \\\\\n&= 4^{n} - 3n \\cdot 4^{n} \\\\\n&= 4^{n}(1 - 3n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186125003 :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x, y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =x+a{y}^{2}-1, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =bxy-5y+3, \\\\ {f}_{x}(1,1) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186125231 :question/problem "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ be a basis of $R^{3}$. The matrix $[T]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T: R^{3} → R^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle [T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{T}({\\mathbf{b}}_{1}) & ={\\mathbf{b}}_{2}-{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}, \\\\ \\mathbf{T}({\\mathbf{b}}_{2}) & ={\\mathbf{b}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}-{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}, \\\\ \\mathbf{T}({\\mathbf{b}}_{3}) & ={\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129046 :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(2\\sqrt{3}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = -1$ into the general solution gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\sqrt{3})^{0}(A\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{0\\cdot 5\\pi }{6}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{0\\cdot 5\\pi }{6}\\right)) & =-1 \\\\ 1\\cdot (A\\cdot 1+B\\cdot 0) & =-1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Substituting $a_1=3+\\sqrt{3}$ and $A=-1$ into the general solution gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\sqrt{3})^{1}(-1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{1\\cdot 5\\pi }{6}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{1\\cdot 5\\pi }{6}\\right)) & =3+\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+B\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) & =3+\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3+\\sqrt{3}B & =3+\\sqrt{3} \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA + B = -1 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= (2\\sqrt{3})^{n}(\\sin \\left(\\frac{5nπ}{6}\\right) - \\cos \\left(\\frac{5nπ}{6}\\right))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129047 :question/worked-solution "Moving all terms of our recurrence relation to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\na_n - 2a_{n-1} + 2a_{n-2} = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 2\\lambda + 2 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 2λ + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 2λ + 1) - 1 + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)^{2} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)^{2} &= -1 \\\\\nλ - 1 &= ± \\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= 1 ± \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 1 + \\textrm{i}, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = 1 - \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nExpressing our solutions in polar form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ_{1} &= \\sqrt{2}(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)), \\\\\nλ_{2} &= \\sqrt{2}(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) - \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(\\sqrt{2}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nWe now find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions:\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = 2$ into the general solution gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{2})^{0}(A\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{0\\cdot \\pi }{4}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{0\\cdot \\pi }{4}\\right)) & =2 \\\\ 1\\cdot (A\\cdot 1+B\\cdot 0) & =2 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Substituting $a_1=5$ and $A=2$ into the general solution gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{2})^{1}(2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{1\\cdot \\pi }{4}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{1\\cdot \\pi }{4}\\right)) & =5 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}+B\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =5 \\\\ 2+B & =5 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(\\sqrt{2}\\big)^n \\left(2\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right) + 3\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\n$$"] [17592186129050 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the general solution $a_n^{(h)}$ of the associated homogeneous difference equation\n\n$$\na_n + 4a_{n-1} + 4a_{n-2} = 0\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 4λ + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 2)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -2\n$$\n\nis a repeated root, and therefore\n\n$$\na_n^{(h)} = A \\cdot (-2)^n + B n \\cdot (-2)^n\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $a_n^{(p)}$ of the original difference equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0$. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0$, i.e.\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} = \\alpha\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined. Since $a_n^{(p)}$ does not depend on $n$, we also have\n\n$$\na_{n-1}^{(p)} = a_{n-2}^{(p)} = \\alpha\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute the particular solution into the difference equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha + 4\\alpha + 4\\alpha = 18 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\alpha = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} = 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{n}^{(h)} + a_{n}^{(p)} \\\\\n&= A \\cdot (-2)^{n} + Bn \\cdot (-2)^{n} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial values.\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = 1$ into the general solution gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot (-2)^{0}+B\\cdot 0\\cdot (-2)^{0}+2 & =1 \\\\ A+2 & =1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Substituting $a_1 = -2$ and $A = -1$ into the general solution gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-2)^{1}+B\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)^{1}+2 & =-2 \\\\ 2-2B+2 & =-2 \\\\ -2B & =-6 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the difference equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= (-1) \\cdot (-2)^{n} + 3n \\cdot (-2)^{n} + 2 \\\\\n&= (-2)^{n}(3n - 1) + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129053 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the general solution $a_n^{(h)}$ of the associated homogeneous difference equation\n\n$$\na_n - 16a_{n-2} = 0\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 16 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 4)(λ - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -4\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na_n^{(h)} = A \\cdot (-4)^n + B \\cdot 4^n\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $a_n^{(p)}$ of the original difference equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous difference equation is $9\\cdot 5^n$. The base of the exponential part is $5$, which is not a root of the characteristic equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} = \\alpha \\cdot 5^n\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant that is to be determined. As a result,\n\n$$\na_{n-1}^{(p)} = \\alpha \\cdot 5^{n-1}, \\qquad a_{n-2}^{(p)} = \\alpha \\cdot 5^{n-2}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute the particular solution into the difference equation and solve for $\\alpha{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\alpha  \\cdot  {5}^{n} - 16 \\cdot  \\alpha  \\cdot  {5}^{n - 2} & \\displaystyle  = 9 \\cdot  {5}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  \\cdot  {5}^{n} - 16 \\alpha  \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {5}^{2}}} \\cdot  {5}^{n} & \\displaystyle  = 9 \\cdot  {5}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  \\cdot  \\cancel{ {5}^{n}} - 16 \\alpha  \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {5}^{2}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{ {5}^{n}} & \\displaystyle  = 9 \\cdot  \\cancel{ {5}^{n}} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  - {\\displaystyle \\frac{16 \\alpha }{25}} & \\displaystyle  = 9 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{9 \\alpha }{25}} & \\displaystyle  = 9 \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  & \\displaystyle  = 25\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} = 25 \\cdot 5^n = 5^{n+2}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{n}^{(h)} + a_{n}^{(p)} \\\\\n&= A \\cdot (-4)^{n} + B \\cdot 4^{n} + 5^{n + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial values.\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = 15$ into the general solution gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =A\\cdot (-4)^{0}+B\\cdot {4}^{0}+{5}^{0+2} \\\\ 15 & =A+B+25 \\\\ A+B & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Substituting $a_1 = 85$ into the general solution gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}85 & =A\\cdot (-4)^{1}+B\\cdot {4}^{1}+{5}^{1+2} \\\\ 85 & =-4A+4B+125 \\\\ -4A+4B & =-40 \\\\ 4A-4B & =40 \\\\ A-B & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}A + B = - 10 \\\\ A - B = 10\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0$, $B=-10$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= -10 \\cdot 4^{n} + 5^{n + 2} \\\\\n&= 5^{n + 2} - 10 \\cdot 4^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186130433 :tutorial/content "When multiplying real numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the product is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The product of two rational numbers is *always* rational since the result must be a fraction of the form $\\dfrac ab$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers.\n- The product of a *nonzero* rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$2\\cdot \\sqrt 2$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the product in decimal form, we get\n$2 \\cdot 1.414213562… = 2.828427125…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following product is irrational:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHowever, the following product is rational:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133558 :question/worked-solution "First, we move the radicals to separate sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} - \\sqrt{x + 6} &= 1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} &= \\sqrt{x + 6} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we raise both sides to the power of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} &= \\sqrt{x + 6} + 1 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{2x + 5})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{x + 6} + 1)^{2} \\\\\n2x + 5 &= (\\sqrt{x + 6})^{2} + 2\\sqrt{x + 6} + 1^{2} \\\\\n2x + 5 &= x + 7 + 2\\sqrt{x + 6} \\\\\n2\\sqrt{x + 6} &= 2x + 5 - x - 7 \\\\\n2\\sqrt{x + 6} &= x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we have an equation with one square root on one side and a linear expression on the other side. To solve this type of equation, we raise both sides to the power of $2$ one more time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\sqrt{x + 6})^{2} &= (x - 2)^{2} \\\\\n4(x + 6) &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\n4x + 24 &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 8x - 20 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 10x + 2x - 20 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 10)(x + 2) &= 0 \\\\\n&= x = 10,-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we check the obtained values by substituting them into the original equation.\n\n- Checking $x=10$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2(10)+5} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{(10)+6}+1 \\\\ \\sqrt{25} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{16}+1 \\\\ 5 & \\overset{?}{=}4+1 \\\\ 5 & =5.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the value $x=10$ is indeed a solution of the original equation\n- Checking $x=-2$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2(-2)+5} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{(-2)+6}+1 \\\\ \\sqrt{1}=1 & \\ne 2+1=3.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe value $x=-2$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt {2x +5} -\\sqrt {x+6} = 1\n$$\n\nhas a single solution $x=10$."] [17592186139670 :tutorial/content "When adding mixed numbers, we sometimes get a result greater than (or equal to) $1$ when adding the fractional parts.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's compute the value of the following sum:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac 2 5 + 3\\,\\dfrac 4 5\n$$\n\nAs before, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions:\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$4+3 = {\\color{red}{7}}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac 2 5 + \\dfrac 4 5 = \\dfrac{2+4}{5} = \\dfrac 6 5.\n$$\nThis is an improper fraction because the numerator $(6)$ is larger than the denominator $(5)$. However, we can convert this improper fraction to a mixed number as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{5} & =6÷5 \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{R}1 \\\\  & ={\\color{blue}{1\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{5}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNotice that this gives us an additional whole number part.\n\nCombining our results, we get\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac 2 5 + 3\\,\\dfrac 4 5 = {\\color{red}{7}} + {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 1 5}} = 8\\,\\dfrac 1 5\n$$"] [17592186142514 :question/problem "Mary wishes to find the value of $4\\times748$. She proceeds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\times 700 & =\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{0000}} \\\\ 4\\times 40 & =\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{000}} \\\\ 4\\times 8 & =\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{00}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the missing numbers, and use your answers to find the value of $4\\times748$."] [17592186142548 :question/problem "Kyle wishes to find the value of $8\\times 62$. He proceeds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\times 60 & =\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{000}} \\\\ 8\\times 2 & =\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{00}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the missing numbers, and use your answers to find the value of $8\\times 62$."] [17592186142552 :question/problem "Ann wishes to find the value of $36\\times 19$. She starts by writing both factors in expanded form.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 36 = {\\color{blue}{30}}+{\\color{blue}{6}},\\qquad 19 = {\\color{red}{10}}+{\\color{red}{9}}\n$$\n\nThen, she proceeds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{30}}\\times {\\color{red}{10}} & =\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{000}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{30}}\\times {\\color{red}{9}} & =\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{000}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{6}}\\times {\\color{red}{10}} & =\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{10}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{6}}\\times {\\color{red}{9}} & =\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the missing numbers, and use your answers to find the value of $36\\times 19$."] [17592186142554 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our factors in expanded form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n83 &= 80 + 3, 47 = 40 + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply *both* numbers in the first factor's expanded form by *both* numbers in the second factor's expanded form:\n\n- First, we multiply ${\\color{blue}{80}}$ by ${\\color{red}{40}}$ and ${\\color{red}{7}}$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{80}}\\times {\\color{red}{40}} & =3,200 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{80}}\\times {\\color{red}{7}} & =560\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Then, we multiply ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ by ${\\color{red}{40}}$ and ${\\color{red}{7}}$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{40}} & =120 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{7}} & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now add all our results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{1}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}9\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n83 \\times 47 = 3,901\n$$"] [17592186142555 :question/worked-solution "To determine the maximum number of people, we need to compute the value of\n\n$$\n68 \\times 36\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write our factors in expanded form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n68 &= 60 + 8, 36 = 30 + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply *both* numbers in the first factor's expanded form by *both* numbers in the second factor's expanded form:\n\n- First, we multiply ${\\color{blue}{60}}$ by ${\\color{red}{30}}$ and ${\\color{red}{6}}$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{60}}\\times {\\color{red}{30}} & =1,800 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{60}}\\times {\\color{red}{6}} & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Then, we multiply ${\\color{blue}{8}}$ by ${\\color{red}{30}}$ and ${\\color{red}{6}}$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{8}}\\times {\\color{red}{30}} & =240 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{8}}\\times {\\color{red}{6}} & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now add all our results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccc} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{1}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{1}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of people that can enter the gym is $2,448$."] [17592186142565 :question/worked-solution "We need to look at the blue and pink rectangles:\n\n- Let's first look at the blue rectangle (the one on the left-hand side). Its area is $350$, and its width is $5$. Therefore, the length of the rectangle must be\n$350 \\div 5$.\nTo carry out this division, we write $350$ as a product with $10$ as one of the factors and then swap the order of multiplication and division:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}350÷5 &= 35\\times 10÷5 \\\\ &= 35÷5\\times 10 \\\\ &= ({\\color{black}{35÷5}})\\times 10 \\\\ &= {\\color{black}{7}}\\times 10 \\\\ &= 70\\end{aligned}\n$$\nLet's add this to our model:![](images/71/713e60dd5e53d3f23ec5ac1d9734719303ba2f0010476095d8dd1d9440082f2e.png)\n- Now, let's look at the pink rectangle (the one on the right-hand side). Its area is $30$, and its width is $5$. Therefore, the length of the rectangle must be\n$30 \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}6}$.\nLet's add this to our model:![](images/f8/f8d62c953300acf23e9e41f39d3fee017b8730fbc7404c297752d3a5b2067546.png)\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing numbers are $70$ and $6$."] [17592186144049 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to use area models to multiply two-digit numbers. Let's now discuss a similar method that does not require models.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n13 \\times 24\n$$\n\nWe begin by expressing both factors in expanded form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n13 &= 10 + 3, 24 = 20 + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply *both* numbers in the first factor's expanded form by *both* numbers in the second factor's expanded form:\n\n- First, we multiply ${\\color{blue}{10}}$ by ${\\color{red}{20}}$ and ${\\color{red}{4}}$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{10}}\\times {\\color{red}{20}} & =200 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{10}}\\times {\\color{red}{4}} & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Then, we multiply ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ by ${\\color{red}{20}}$ and ${\\color{red}{4}}$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{20}} & =60 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{4}} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now add all our results:\n\n$$\n1; 2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ + & 4\\end{vmatrix}0; +\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ + & 1\\end{vmatrix}2; 3 \\mid 1 \\mid 2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n13 \\times 24 = 312\n$$\n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare this computation to the corresponding area model.\n\n![](images/bf/bf0971653e178afbc398fbfb8c783932adbb939ace0ff1767e7f933d3bc1c848.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The intermediate products $(200,40,60$, and $12)$ we found during our calculation all correspond to the areas of small rectangles in the area model.\n- Summing these intermediate products corresponds to finding the area of the large rectangle by adding the areas of the small rectangles."] [17592186147171 :question/worked-solution "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2$ with multiplication signs:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{{\\color{blue}{2}}} & =\\underbrace{\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{5}}_{{\\color{blue}{2}}\\text{ times}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\times 4}{5\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2= \\dfrac{16}{25}\n$$"] [17592186147172 :question/worked-solution "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3$ with multiplication signs:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{{\\color{blue}{3}}} & =\\underbrace{\\frac{3}{5}\\times \\frac{3}{5}\\times \\frac{3}{5}}_{{\\color{blue}{3}}\\text{ times}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{3} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\times \\frac{3}{5}\\times \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 3\\times 3}{5\\times 5\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\times 3}{25\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\dfrac{27}{125}}\n$$"] [17592186147187 :question/worked-solution "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4$ with multiplication signs:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^{\\color{blue}{4}} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8}}_{\\large { { {\\color{blue}4}} \\text{ times}}}\n$$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{4} & =\\frac{3}{8}\\times \\frac{3}{8}\\times \\frac{3}{8}\\times \\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}{8\\times 8\\times 8\\times 8} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\overbrace{3\\times 3}^{9}\\times \\overbrace{3\\times 3}^{9}}{\\underbrace{8\\times 8}_{64}\\times \\underbrace{8\\times 8}_{64}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\times 9}{64\\times 64} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4\\hspace{0.167em}096}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{81}{4\\,096}}\n$$"] [17592186147193 :question/worked-solution "Recall that on a box plot,\n\n- the smallest value in the data corresponds to the left whisker, and\n- the greatest value corresponds to the right whisker.\n\nThe range of a dataset is the difference between the greatest and the smallest values.\n\nWith that in mind, let's find the ranges of the given datasets.\n\n- Consider the box plot corresponding to the first week.![](images/12/12a2d26dfb33ddaae846ca332f5a684dca70720af005543aedc7e501192890e8.png)\nHere, we have the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Smallest} = 2 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\text{Greatest} = 11\n$$\nSo, the range of the dataset is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Range} & =\\text{Greatest}-\\text{Smallest} \\\\  & =11-2 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider the box plot corresponding to the second week.![](images/01/01d1ab0ff1e0aa7533e1dd0b7af5efaae56f0034b20c075e6d8c7e9367beb0c6.png)\nHere, we have the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Smallest} = 1 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\text{Greatest} = 15\n$$\nSo, the range of the dataset is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Range} & =\\text{Greatest}-\\text{Smallest} \\\\  & =15-1 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $9 < 14$, a comparison of the ranges suggests that the dataset corresponding to the second week is more spread out."] [17592186147194 :question/worked-solution "The interquartile range (IQR) of a dataset is the difference between the upper and the lower quartiles.\n\nWith that in mind, let's find the interquartile ranges of the given datasets.\n\n- Consider the dot plot corresponding to the first assistant.Notice that the number of data points ($15$) is odd. As a result, we can separate the data into its lower and upper parts, as illustrated in the diagram below.![](images/e0/e0966bd1467619b0235b410adf7bed82b6ea862cd128242b6f584dedf1c6e192.png)\nHere, we have the following:The lower quartile is the median of the lower part. In this case, it's just the middle number. So, the lower quartile is\n$\\text{Lower Quartile} = 21$.The upper quartile is the median of the upper part. In this case, it's just the middle number. So, the upper quartile is\n$\\text{Upper Quartile} = 23$.Thus, the interquartile range is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{IQR} & =\\text{Upper Quartile}-\\text{Lower Quartile} \\\\  & =23-21 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider the dot plot corresponding to the second assistant.Notice that the number of data points ($12$) is even. As a result, we can separate the data into its lower and upper parts, as illustrated in the diagram below.![](images/cf/cf9f14b1ec079ae949177dec31c679a10a8dc44c07dc4e6309d5d5065df1871f.png)\nHere, we have the following:The lower quartile is the median of the lower part. In this case, it's the average of the two middle numbers. So, the lower quartile is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Lower Quartile} = \\dfrac{21+22}{2} = \\dfrac{43}{2} = 21.5.\n$$The upper quartile is the median of the upper part. In this case, it's the average of the two middle numbers. So, the upper quartile is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Upper Quartile} = \\dfrac{23+23}{2} = \\dfrac{46}{2} = 23.\n$$Thus, the interquartile range is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{IQR} & =\\text{Upper Quartile}-\\text{Lower Quartile} \\\\  & =23-21.5 \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2 > 1.5$, a comparison of the interquartile ranges suggests that the dataset corresponding to the first assistant is more spread out."]}
